 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Temperature tells how hot or cold something is. It is measured with a thermometer.\nUnits: degrees Celsius (°C) in daily life; the SI unit is the kelvin (K). T(K) = T(°C) + 273. Water freezes at 0 °C (273 K) and boils at 100 °C (373 K).\nA liquid-in-glass thermometer has mercury or coloured alcohol, which expands when warmed. Read it at eye level. A clinical thermometer measures body temperature (normal about 37 °C).\nEffects of temperature on matter: heating makes most things expand (get bigger) and cooling makes them contract; a big enough change in temperature changes the state (melting, boiling).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "t": 37,
   "k": 273
  },
  "problem": "Normal body temperature is {t} °C. Write it in kelvin.",
  "steps": [
   "T(K) = T(°C) + {k}.",
   "{t} + {k} = {toKelvin(t)} K."
  ],
  "answer": "{toKelvin(t)} K",
  "checks": [
   "toKelvin(t) == 310"
  ]
 },
